            "The northern half of Ghana — the Upper East, Upper West, Northern, North East and Savannah Regions, with the Bono East and Oti Regions on the fringe — lies in the African <strong>meningitis belt</strong>, where the dry harmattan months from December to May dry and crack the nasopharyngeal mucosa, crowd families indoors against the dust and let <em>Neisseria meningitidis</em> and <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, carried harmlessly in the throats of many, invade the bloodstream and the meninges of a few in seasonal epidemics that once killed one patient in five. The meningococcal A epidemics have ended since the MenAfriVac campaign of 2012 and the conjugate vaccine's place at eighteen months in the schedule, but serogroups W, X and C and, above all, the <strong>pneumococcus</strong> now fill the gap — in the Upper West outbreak of early 2025, with over two hundred suspected cases and seventeen deaths, the pneumococcus was the commonest confirmed organism. Outside the belt, meningitis occurs all year: pneumococcal and meningococcal in children and adults, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> type b now rare thanks to the Pentavalent, group B streptococcus, <em>E. coli</em> and <em>Listeria</em> in the newborn, and the tuberculous and cryptococcal meningitis of lesson 3.2 in the patient with HIV. The surveillance thresholds decide the response: an <strong>alert</strong> at three suspected cases per 100,000 population in a week (or two cases in a week in a population under thirty thousand), which triggers investigation, laboratory confirmation of the first cases and readiness; an <strong>epidemic</strong> at ten per 100,000 in a week (five in a district at high risk or already in alert), which triggers the epidemic treatment protocol and a reactive vaccination campaign with the vaccine that matches the confirmed serogroup — now including the pentavalent conjugate vaccine against A, C, W, X and Y.",
