            "Meningitis is treated on suspicion. The <strong>first dose of ceftriaxone</strong> — 100 mg/kg (maximum 2 g twice daily, or 4 g once in the epidemic protocol) intravenously, or intramuscularly if there is no vein — is given within thirty minutes of the patient arriving, after a blood culture if that costs no time and before the lumbar puncture if the puncture will take longer than that; a nurse at a CHPS compound who suspects meningitis gives it before the referral vehicle leaves. The <strong>lumbar puncture</strong> is performed when it is safe: it is deferred when there are signs of raised intracranial pressure (a falling or very low conscious level, focal weakness, unequal or fixed pupils, papilloedema, bradycardia with hypertension and irregular breathing), shock, a bleeding tendency or infection at the site, because withdrawing fluid below a pressurised brain can cone the brainstem. The <strong>cerebrospinal fluid</strong> of bacterial meningitis is turbid or purulent, with hundreds to thousands of white cells, mostly neutrophils, a protein above 1 g/L and a glucose below two-thirds of the blood glucose (often below 2.2 mmol/L); it goes for Gram stain, culture, latex agglutination and PCR, and in an epidemic the first ten to twenty samples are confirmed to identify the serogroup. Ceftriaxone continues for five days in epidemic meningococcal meningitis, ten to fourteen for pneumococcal and other causes, with ampicillin added for the newborn and the elderly against <em>Listeria</em>; oily chloramphenicol is the alternative where ceftriaxone is unavailable. <strong>Dexamethasone</strong> before or with the first antibiotic dose reduces deafness and death in pneumococcal meningitis in adults and children and is given where the organism is likely to be pneumococcal, but not in the epidemic meningococcal setting.",
